      "text": "Let $u = \\frac{x}{y}$, where $u$ and $x$ are both positive.  Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} &= \\frac{(yu + y)^2}{y^2u^2 + y^2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(yu + y)^2}{y^2(u^2 + 1)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(y(u + 1))^2}{y^2(u^2 + 1)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(u + 1)^2}{u^2 + 1},\n\\end{align*}so $\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}$ is maximized when $(u + 1)^2$ is maximized.  Thus, the maximum value of $\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}$ is $\\frac{2^2}{1^2} = \\boxed{4}$. The answer is 4",
